<main>
 <section class="guide-hero"><p class="eyebrow">THE PLUGIN MAKER’S GUIDE</p><h1>Your idea.<br /><span>Inside Mentos.</span></h1><p>Start with a few lines of JavaScript. Add a command, make a native page, or connect a tool to your network. No Flutter build required.</p><div class="hero-links"><a class="button primary" href="#start">Build your first plugin ↓</a><a href="/plugins/hello-starter.js" download>Download the starter .js ↗</a></div></section>
 <div class="guide-layout"><aside><p class="eyebrow">ON THIS PAGE</p><nav aria-label="Guide contents">{#each sections as [id,title],i}<a href={`#${id}`}><span>0{i+1}</span>{title}</a>{/each}</nav><a class="source" href="https://github.com/dagmawibabi/mentos/blob/main/plugins/README.md">App API documentation ↗</a></aside>
 <div class="chapters">
 <section id="start"><p class="eyebrow">01 / START SMALL</p><h2>A command in one file.</h2><p>Save this as <code>hello.js</code>. The leading <code>@plugin</code> comment is its manifest: it identifies the plugin and declares the permissions it needs.</p><CodeBlock code={example.hello} label="hello.js — complete installable plugin" /><ol><li>Move the file to your phone.</li><li>In Mentos, open <strong>Settings → Plugins → + → Install from file</strong>.</li><li>Review the commands permission and install.</li><li>In a chat, submit <code>/hello Ada</code>. Mentos sends <strong>Hello, Ada!</strong> to that conversation.</li></ol><div class="note">A command’s return value is outgoing message text. Return <code>''</code> for a local action that should send nothing.</div></section>
 <section id="structure"><p class="eyebrow">02 / GIVE IT A HOME</p><h2>Two files. One identity.</h2><p>For a reusable package, put a manifest and script in a folder. This second example is a persistent counter.</p><CodeBlock code={example.tree} label="Repository layout" /><CodeBlock code={example.manifest} label="plugins/my-counter/plugin.json" /><p>Use a unique ID with 2–40 lowercase letters, digits, or dashes; it must match the folder name. IDs are shared across registries, so avoid taking an existing one. Bump the version when releasing an update.</p><p>Use <code>main.js</code> for this site’s generator. Supported icons include <code>calculate</code>, <code>note</code>, <code>chat</code>, <code>timer</code>, <code>code</code>, <code>moon</code>, and <code>sun</code>. Unknown icons use a puzzle piece.</p></section>
 <section id="pages"><p class="eyebrow">03 / BUILD SOMETHING USEFUL</p><h2>A page that remembers.</h2><p>Use this script with the counter manifest above. Mentos renders the nodes as native widgets and rerenders after UI callbacks. Storage survives plugin restarts on this device.</p><CodeBlock code={example.counter} label="plugins/my-counter/main.js" /><p>Open the installed plugin’s page and tap Add one. Restart Mentos and verify the count remains. If a timer or network callback changes page state, call <code>mentos.ui.refresh()</code>.</p><h3>Native UI building blocks</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Node</th><th>Fields</th></tr></thead><tbody>{#each nodes as [node,fields]}<tr><td><code>{node}</code></td><td>{fields}</td></tr>{/each}</tbody></table></div><p>A string is shorthand for a text node; <code>null</code> is skipped. Render functions return an array of nodes. Keep render functions focused on drawing UI; start network requests and timers outside them.</p></section>
 <section id="extend"><p class="eyebrow">04 / FEEL AT HOME</p><h2>Cards, tabs, and your own look.</h2><p>With the <code>ui</code> permission, add a card to an existing screen or pin a registered page to the bottom navigation. This example extends the counter above.</p><CodeBlock code={example.slots} /><p>Available slots: <code>feed.top</code>, <code>chats.top</code>, <code>profile.top</code>, <code>wallet.top</code>, and <code>network.top</code>. Return <code>null</code> to hide a card.</p><h3>Theme and layout</h3><p>The following snippet needs both <code>theme</code> and <code>layout</code> added to the manifest’s permissions. Disabling or removing a layout plugin restores the app layout.</p><CodeBlock code={example.theme} /><p>Layout also supports hiding <code>stories</code>, <code>composeButton</code>, <code>quickActions</code>, or <code>marketBanner</code>. Theme colors include <code>bg</code>, <code>surface</code>, <code>ink</code>, <code>muted</code>, <code>accent</code>, and chat bubble colors.</p></section>
 <section id="connect"><p class="eyebrow">05 / CONNECT YOUR TOOL</p><h2>APIs and peer messages.</h2><h3>HTTPS requests · net permission</h3><p>Use the callback-based HTTP API. Replace the example URL with your own HTTPS endpoint; it is a placeholder, not a working service.</p><CodeBlock code={example.network} /><p>The response contains <code>ok, status, headers, body, json, ms, error</code>. For other methods, use <code>mentos.http.request</code> with <code>url, method, headers, body, timeout</code>. Handle failure in the callback.</p><h3>Peer-to-peer · p2p permission</h3><p>Both contacts must have the same plugin installed. Use <code>mentos.peers.list()</code> to populate a contact picker, then send structured data to the selected peer.</p><CodeBlock code={example.peers} /><p>Peer messages are end-to-end encrypted and limited to 32 KB. Validate received data before acting on it. They are plugin messages, separate from ordinary chat messages.</p></section>
 <section id="reference"><p class="eyebrow">06 / KNOW YOUR TOOLS</p><h2>Permissions & API reference.</h2><p>All calls below start with <code>mentos.</code>. Declare only the permissions your script uses. Installation asks the person to approve them; accessing an undeclared API fails.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Permission</th><th>API / behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody>{#each apis as [permission,api,detail]}<tr><td><code>{permission}</code></td><td><code>{api}</code><p>{detail}</p></td></tr>{/each}</tbody></table></div><h3>A JavaScript subset, inside a sandbox</h3><p>Use variables, functions, closures, arrays, objects, loops, template strings, optional chaining, and common String, Array, Math, JSON, Object, and Date.now helpers. Browser and Node APIs are unavailable: no imports, DOM, eval, direct filesystem access, or global fetch. Use the Mentos APIs instead.</p><p>Execution has a step budget. Keep callbacks short; use timers for delayed work. Timers run while the plugin runtime is active, so do not promise background or closed-app reminders.</p></section>
 <section id="test"><p class="eyebrow">07 / TRY IT FOR REAL</p><h2>Install. Inspect. Iterate.</h2><p>Open <strong>Plugins → your plugin → Log</strong> to inspect errors and <code>mentos.log</code> output. Test the behavior in the actual app; passing a browser JavaScript check does not prove compatibility with the interpreter.</p><ul><li>Exercise commands and every button, including empty or invalid input.</li><li>Restart the app to check persistence; disable the plugin to check cleanup.</li><li>Check both themes and long labels on a narrow screen.</li><li>For HTTP and peer tools, test unreachable services and missing peers.</li></ul><h3>Develop against a running app</h3><p>Enable <strong>Settings → Developer</strong>. The dashboard at <code>http://127.0.0.1:8787</code> includes plugin logs, reload, and enable controls. Push a single-file plugin using the app’s access code:</p><CodeBlock code={example.curl} label="Terminal — replace the access-code placeholder" /><p>Run this where the app’s local developer endpoint is reachable. On a separate phone, localhost refers to the phone; use your existing device forwarding setup. Keep the access code out of published files.</p></section>
 <section id="publish"><p class="eyebrow">08 / SHARE YOUR WORK</p><h2>From your device to Discover.</h2><p>Add the plugin folder to either repository, then regenerate that repository’s index.</p><CodeBlock code="npm run plugins:build" label="In the mentossite repository" /><CodeBlock code="dart run plugins/build.dart" label="In the mentos app repository" /><p>Publish the manifest, script, and generated <code>plugins/index.json</code> together. This site reads the registries from both <code>dagmawibabi/mentos</code> and <code>dagmawibabi/mentossite</code>. A plugin is not listed remotely until its registry entry is published.</p><div class="note"><strong>Keep the bytes consistent.</strong> Use LF line endings and regenerate the index after any script change. The SHA-256 pin must match the exact published script. This site’s generator normalizes line endings; do not edit generated checksums by hand.</div><p>For updates, increase the manifest version, rebuild the registry, and publish both. Discover offers the update and highlights new permissions. Test an install from the published registry as well as your local file.</p><h3>Host your own registry</h3><p>Serve an index over HTTPS. Each entry includes the manifest metadata, script SHA-256, and a <code>manifest</code> path relative to the index URL. Users add it in <strong>Plugins → + → Registries</strong>.</p><a class="button primary" href="/plugins">Explore working plugins ↗</a></section>
 </div></div>
</main><footer><a class="brand" href="/">mentos.</a><p>Make something that feels like you.</p><a href="/plugins">Plugin collection ↗</a></footer></div>
